@page "/ai"

<PageTitle>AI agents and MCP · RioCharts</PageTitle>

<h1>AI agents and MCP</h1>
<p class="lead">
    <code>RioCharts.AI</code> gives agents a <code>create_chart</code> tool. The agent writes a small <a href="specs">JSON spec</a> against your datasets;
    the tool validates it, renders it on the server and returns a short description, or errors that name the path and the valid options so the
    model can fix the spec and call again. The image goes to your app; <code>render_chart</code> returns it to the agent instead.
</p>

<h2>Install</h2>
<CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="dotnet add package RioCharts.AI --prerelease" />
<p>It brings in <code>RioCharts.Skia</code> for the images. For a local agent with no app of your own, use <a href="ai#local-agents">riocharts-mcp</a> instead.</p>

<h2>Your data</h2>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@DataCode" />
<p>The tool description lists each dataset with its fields, types and descriptions, the spec format and a few examples. Querying and access control stay in your app.</p>

<h2>Microsoft.Extensions.AI</h2>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@AiCode" />

<h2>MCP server</h2>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@McpCode" />
<p>Tools: <code>create_chart</code> (returns text and a PNG image), <code>render_chart</code> (PNG or SVG for the agent), <code>list_datasets</code> and <code>get_chart_schema</code>.</p>

<h2>Images for the agent</h2>
<p>
    <code>create_chart</code> shows a chart to the user. When the agent needs the image itself, for a report, an email or a document,
    it calls <code>render_chart</code> with the same spec plus <code>format</code> (<code>png</code> or <code>svg</code>), <code>width</code>,
    <code>height</code> and <code>theme</code>. The image comes back as content: <code>DataContent</code> with Microsoft.Extensions.AI, an image block
    (PNG) or an embedded <code>image/svg+xml</code> resource (SVG) over MCP.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@RenderCode" />

<h2 id="local-agents">Local agents: riocharts-mcp</h2>
<p>
    For agents on your machine, such as Claude Code, Claude Desktop or GitHub Copilot in VS Code, there is a ready-made MCP server. It runs over stdio,
    so the agent starts it; you write no host app. CSV and JSON files in the <code>--data</code> folder become datasets (read again when they change),
    and every chart is saved to <code>--out</code> as PNG or SVG, with the path returned to the agent. Tools: <code>render_chart</code>,
    <code>list_datasets</code> and <code>get_chart_schema</code>. It runs on Windows (x64, arm64, x86), macOS and Linux (x64 and arm64, glibc and musl).
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@ToolInstallCode" />
<CodeBlock Language="json" Code="@ToolConfigCode" />
<p>
    Options: <code>--data</code>, <code>--out</code> (default <code>./charts</code>), <code>--width</code>, <code>--height</code>,
    <code>--theme light|dark</code> and <code>--culture</code> (e.g. <code>sv-SE</code>). CSV files may use comma, semicolon or tab, and a decimal comma
    with semicolons, as Excel writes them.
</p>

<h2>From chat to code</h2>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@RazorCode" />

@code {
    private const string DataCode = """
        // Datasets the agent may chart: from rows, objects or JSON.
        var shop = ChartDataset.FromItems("shop", traffic, "Store traffic per sales channel, hourly",
            new Dictionary<string, string> { ["orders"] = "orders per hour", ["revenue"] = "revenue per hour, €" });
        var source = new InMemoryDataSource([shop]);   // or implement IChartDataSource to load on demand
        var tool = new ChartTool(source, new ChartToolOptions { Export = new ExportOptions { Width = 720, Height = 360 } });
        tool.ChartCreated += result => ShowToUser(result.Png!, result.Summary);
        """;

    private const string AiCode = """
        IChatClient client = ...;
        var options = new ChatOptions { Tools = tool.AsAIFunctions() };
        var response = await client.GetResponseAsync("Chart orders per channel this week, with our goal of 40 an hour", options);

        // The model calls create_chart with e.g.
        // {"dataset":"shop","series":[{"type":"line","x":"timestamp","y":"orders","groupBy":"channel"}],
        //  "annotations":[{"value":40,"label":"Goal"}]}
        """;

    private const string McpCode = """
        // dotnet add package ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore
        builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
            .WithHttpTransport()
            .WithRioChartsTools(tool);

        app.MapMcp("/mcp");
        """;

    private const string RenderCode = """
        // Optional: also save each image to a folder and return its path (for local agents).
        var tool = new ChartTool(source, new ChartToolOptions { OutputDirectory = "charts" });

        // The model calls render_chart with e.g.
        // {"spec":{"dataset":"shop","title":"Revenue per day","series":[{"type":"bar","x":"timestamp","y":"revenue","timeBucket":"day","aggregate":"sum"}]},
        //  "format":"svg","width":640,"height":320}

        // Or from code:
        var result = await tool.RenderChartAsync(json, new ChartImageRequest { Format = ChartImageFormat.Png, Width = 640, Height = 320 });
        byte[] png = result.Png!;
        """;

    private const string ToolInstallCode = """
        dotnet tool install -g RioCharts.Mcp --prerelease
        # later, to get a newer version:
        dotnet tool update -g RioCharts.Mcp --prerelease
        """;

    private const string ToolConfigCode = """
        // .mcp.json (Claude Code), or the MCP settings of your agent
        {
          "mcpServers": {
            "riocharts": { "command": "riocharts-mcp", "args": ["--data", "data", "--out", "charts"] }
          }
        }
        """;

    private const string RazorCode = """
        // Turn a spec the agent built into component markup for the codebase:
        string razor = ChartRazor.FromSpec(ChartSpecJson.Parse(json).Spec!, itemType: "Traffic", itemsName: "traffic");
        """;
}
